{"name":"Retreat","hex":"#7a8076","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Retreat","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A locality in the Tamworth council area, north eastern New South Wales, Australia.","A locality in George Town council area and the City of Launceston, northern Tasmania, Australia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Retreat"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bugle call","blocks":[{"text":"A bugle call is a short tune, originating as a military signal announcing scheduled and certain non-scheduled events on a military installation, battlefield, or ship. Historically, bugles, drums, and other loud musical instruments were used for clear communication in the noise and confusion of a battlefield. Naval bugle calls were also used to command the crew of many warships."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/Bugler_of_the_French_Marine_Infantry%2C_Kuwait_City%2C_1991.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bugle_call"},{"title":"Withdrawal (military)","blocks":[{"text":"A tactical withdrawal or retreating defensive action is a type of military operation, generally meaning that retreating forces draw back while maintaining contact with the enemy. A withdrawal may be undertaken as part of a general retreat, to consolidate forces, to occupy ground that is more easily defended, force the enemy to overextend to secure a decisive victory, or to lead the enemy into an ambush. It is considered a relatively risky operation, requiring discipline to keep from turning into a disorganized rout or at the very least doing severe damage to the military's morale."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Napoleons_retreat_from_Moscow_by_Adolph_Northen.jpg/330px-Napoleons_retreat_from_Moscow_by_Adolph_Northen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Withdrawal_(military)"},{"title":"Retreat (survivalism)","blocks":[{"text":"In the survivalist subculture or movement, a retreat is a place of refuge. Sometimes their retreats are called a bug-out location (BOL), a bunker, or a bolt hole. Survivalist retreats are intended to be self-sufficient and easily defended. Generally, they are located in sparsely populated outback rural areas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Fort_Furggels_-_Bunker_North.jpg/330px-Fort_Furggels_-_Bunker_North.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Retreat_(survivalism)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Memorial_Stained_Glass_window%2C_Class_of_1934%2C_Royal_Military_College_of_Canada.jpg/250px-Memorial_Stained_Glass_window%2C_Class_of_1934%2C_Royal_Military_College_of_Canada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Memorial_Stained_Glass_window%2C_Class_of_1934%2C_Royal_Military_College_of_Canada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Memorial_Stained_Glass_window%2C_Class_of_1934%2C_Royal_Military_College_of_Canada.jpg/250px-Memorial_Stained_Glass_window%2C_Class_of_1934%2C_Royal_Military_College_of_Canada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Memorial Stained Glass window, Class of 1934, Royal Military College of Canada showing Officer Cadet playing the Bugle call for Last Post or The Rouse","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMemorial_Stained_Glass_window%2C_Class_of_1934%2C_Royal_Military_College_of_Canada.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Trumpetcallsa.jpg/500px-Trumpetcallsa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content